Begin with the question, object or phenomenon that gives the reader a reason to continue.

## The central idea

Develop the mathematical story here. Distinguish clearly between experiment, computation, conjecture and proof.

## A question to try

Give the reader a meaningful point of entry before resolving the whole argument.

## Where to continue

Use compact related-entry cards with a type-and-title line and a brief description. Do not use a plain bulleted link list.

## Article information

Add visible keywords, verified primary and secondary MSC2020 classifications, a preferred citation, and a BibLaTeX record. Remove `draft: true` only after checking every link, image description, KaTeX expression, source, canonical URL, and machine-readable record.
